cal and  as opposed to any sort of heteronomy on  the will so let's talk about what he  goes over in this section again he  distinguishes between a world of sense  and a world of the understanding or the  intelligible world uses these fairly  synonymously rightly so and within the  world of sense we see ourselves as  beings that do in fact carry out actions  but these actions are effects or is  going to call them phenomena of the will  which is a free causality not something  that we can directly observe through  experience in the world of sense so it  not something that is say the object of  science you know or the object of  empirical observation but rather it's a  free causality and he's discussed this  quite a bit in other places so you can  look at the other videos talking about  that  and so the will as frequently is part of  the intelligible world why is that  important well insofar he says as our  actions are stemming from this will this  free causality the actions themselves  are effects determined by that cause  however we understand ourselves as  phenomenal beings as beings that have  desires and inclinations as well all of  us have these we have a whole panoply of  desires and inclinations these can be  anything under the Sun that's why I  can't says at another place that you  can't really determine what happiness is  because it's a satisfaction of desires  and inclinations there's so many of them  who knows you know what which ones any  given human being has however these  desires and inclinations they can also  determine our actions when we're  following along by say a psychological  determinism when we're behaving in  immoral or non moral ways we're allowing  desires and inclinations to you might  say run the show to to make the final  decision about what we're going to do  and Khan says that we can even talk  about a law or laws plural of desires  and inclinations these are exactly  what's a psychological laws are supposed  to be they pertain to the 